From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Jon Pretty <jon.pretty@propensive.com>
Date: Sun, 15 Nov 2026 15:44:57 +0900
Subject: Strip capture sets when normalizing the atoms of a singleton

Under capture checking a singleton did not conform to a union containing it,
when the singleton was a member of an object nested anywhere but in a package:

    object P:
      val k1 = Kind()
      val k2 = Kind()
      val e: k1.type = k1
      val f: k1.type | k2.type = e

    Found:    (P.e : (P.k1 : Kind^{}))
    Required: (P.k1 : Kind^{}) | (P.k2 : Kind^{})

A subtype test against a union of singletons is first decided by the
singletons' atoms, which normalize a prefix `this` of an object to the object
itself, by following its underlying type while it is a singleton. A union
caches its atoms for the run, so those of `k1.type | k2.type` were found
while typing, where `this` of `P` is `P.type`. While capture checking, Setup
has given `P`'s class a self type with a capture variable, `P.type^{'s}`,
which is not a singleton, so normalization stopped there, and the atom of
`e` kept the prefix `this`. The atoms differed, and the comparison answered
false outright, without trying the members of the union.

A capture set does not change which value a singleton is, so it is now
stripped from the underlying type while normalizing. An object in a package
was unaffected because its prefix is the package.

Written with LLM assistance (Claude Code).

Co-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5.5 <noreply@anthropic.com>
Proscala-Patch: unionatoms
